and so forth. He thinks as follows: for one for whom the universal is not cognized without inference, let this defect apply;
but for us, the universal is cognized even through a conceptual cognition that follows perception (<s>pratyakṣapṛṣṭhabhāvin</s>), through a natural confusion (<s>prakṛtivibhrama</s>). And since the conceptualization of the logical mark is grounded in the seeing of the particular (<s>svalakṣaṇa</s>), indirectly, through the constraint by reality, it is non-deceptive — like the mistaking [of a jewel] for a gem in [seeing] a gem's luster. The causal efficacy of the smoke-universal, which appears in conceptualization, being established as a logical mark, [arises] from its being grounded in the observation of the effect and from the determinate judgment of that. For it is not proper to establish the particularity of smoke itself as the logical mark, since being unique it would lack occurrence in the positive instances (<s>sapakṣa</s>), since there could be no pervasion of a portion of it, and since in the construction of the provable and the prover, the seeing of the reality [in question] would be impossible.
As for what was stated — 'inference has a universal-characterized object' (<s>sāmānyalakṣaṇaviṣaya</s>) — it is not determined thus: 'only inference has a universal-characterized object.' For even a conceptual cognition that follows perception has that as its object, as does other conceptualization. Rather, what is determined is: 'inference has only a universal-characterized object' — this is for the purpose of denying that it has a particular-characterized object. In that [phrase], 'that which exists together with smoke' (<s>saha dhūmena vartate</s>) is:

